    {
      "id": "630mkl8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.2 Physical Divisions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1e5hpxp",
      "content": "Madhya Pradesh has diverse physiographic features that can be divided into several major regions. The state encompasses plateaus, river valleys, hill ranges, and plains."
    },
    {
      "id": "0qu6v13",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Major Physical Regions"
    },
    {
      "id": "9hphqvy",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Malwa Plateau - Located in western MP, average elevation 300-600m, black cotton soil (regur), major rivers Chambal and Betwa",
        "Central Highlands / Vindhyan Plateau - Runs east-west across central MP, part of the Vindhyan Range",
        "Narmada Valley - The fertile valley of the Narmada River dividing peninsular India from the Deccan",
        "Satpura Range - Runs parallel to Narmada in southern MP, Pachmarhi (highest peak Dhupgarh 1,350m) is located here",
        "Bundelkhand Plateau - Located in northern and northeastern MP, known for its rugged terrain",
        "Baghelkhand Plateau - Located in eastern MP, part of the Son-Narmada divide",
        "Chhattisgarh Plain (formerly part of MP) - Now in Chhattisgarh"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4a4u66c",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Satpura Range"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8u7y8ao",
      "content": "The Satpura Range runs parallel to the Narmada River in southern Madhya Pradesh. It extends from the Arabian Sea coast in Gujarat to the Mahanadi watershed in Chhattisgarh. The Satpura National Park and Pachmarhi Biosphere Reserve are located in this range. Pachmarhi is known as the 'Queen of Satpura' and is the only hill station of Madhya Pradesh. Dhupgarh (1,350 m) near Pachmarhi is the highest peak of Madhya Pradesh."
    },
    {
      "id": "2dvuksy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Vindhyan Range"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "er2bizd",
      "content": "The Vindhyan Range forms the northern escarpment of the Deccan Plateau and runs across central Madhya Pradesh. It separates the Gangetic plains from the Deccan plateau. The range contains rich mineral deposits, particularly sandstone, limestone, and diamonds. Panna district, famous for its diamond mines, is located in the Vindhyan region."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "River Systems of Madhya Pradesh",
      "id": "czg7h06"
    },
    {
      "id": "l8hsq70",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.3 River Systems of Madhya Pradesh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fp2d2ag",
      "content": "Madhya Pradesh is the source of several major rivers of India, earning it the title of 'River Origin State' or 'Mother of Rivers.' Most of the rivers in northern India have their origins in the plateaus and hills of MP."
    },
    {
      "id": "i3a539s",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Narmada River"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wt9q3n2",
      "content": "The Narmada is the most important river of Madhya Pradesh. It originates from Amarkantak (Anuppur district) at an elevation of 1057 metres and flows westward through a rift valley between the Vindhyan and Satpura ranges. The total length of Narmada is 1,312 km, of which about 1,077 km flows through Madhya Pradesh. It finally meets the Arabian Sea in Gujarat. The Narmada is considered sacred and is often called 'Rewa' (meaning 'swift') in ancient texts. The Jabalpur area is famous for the 'Marble Rocks' (Bhedaghat) on the Narmada."
    },
